+##### onRetry(context)
+
+Type: `Function`
+
+Called only when another attempt is actually going to happen, right before the wait. It receives the same context as `onFailedAttempt`, so `retryDelay` is the time about to be waited. Unlike `onFailedAttempt`, it is not called for the final failure, which makes it suited to counting retries in metrics.
+
+The function is called _after_ `onFailedAttempt`, `shouldConsumeRetry` and `shouldRetry`. It is also called when `shouldConsumeRetry` returned `false`, since that retry is attempted straight away.
+
+The function is _not_ called when the error is thrown instead of retried: an `AbortError`, an abort through `signal`, a `TypeError` (except network errors), `shouldRetry` returning `false`, or `retries` or `maxRetryTime` being exhausted.
+
+The function can return a promise, which is awaited before the wait starts. If it throws, all retries will be aborted and the original promise will reject with the thrown error.
+
+```js
+import pRetry from 'p-retry';
+
+const run = async () => { … };
+
+const result = await pRetry(run, {
+	onRetry: ({error, attemptNumber}) => {
+		metrics.increment('retries');
+	}
+});
+```
+
 ##### shouldRetry(context)
 
 Type: `Function`
@@ -224,6 +248,26 @@ Measured with a monotonic clock (`performance.now()`) so system clock adjustment
 
 The limit is checked after failed attempts and callbacks, before scheduling a retry. The retry delay is shortened to the remaining time, but the scheduled retry can still start after the limit. Attempts and callbacks are never interrupted, so they can run past the limit.
 
+##### initialDelay
+
+Type: `number`\
+Default: `0`
+
+The number of milliseconds to wait before the first attempt. The default of `0` starts right away, without a timer.
+
+If `signal` aborts during the wait, the promise rejects with the abort reason and `input` is never called. A fractional value rounds up to a whole millisecond, and the delay is capped at 2147483647ms like the retry delays. `unref` applies to this wait too.
+
+The `maxRetryTime` clock starts after this wait, so it does not eat into the time retrying is allowed to take.
+
+```js
+import pRetry from 'p-retry';
+
+const run = async () => { … };
+
+// Give a service that is still starting up a moment before the first call.
+const result = await pRetry(run, {initialDelay: 2000});
+```
+
 ##### signal
 
 Type: [`AbortSignal`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortSignal)
